       named‐journalprint - print zone journal in human‐readable form

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       nnaammeedd--jjoouurrnnaallpprriinntt [-c serial] [--dduuxx] {journal}

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       nnaammeedd--jjoouurrnnaallpprriinntt  scans the contents of a zone journal file, printing
       it in a human-readable form, or, optionally, converting it to a differ‐
       ent journal file format.

       Journal files are automatically created by _n_a_m_e_d when changes are  made
       to  dynamic  zones  (e.g.,  by  _n_s_u_p_d_a_t_e). They record each addition or
       deletion of a resource record, in binary format, allowing  the  changes
       to be re-applied to the zone when the server is restarted after a shut‐
       down  or  crash.  By default, the name of the journal file is formed by
       appending the extension ..jjnnll to the  name  of  the  corresponding  zone
       file.

       nnaammeedd--jjoouurrnnaallpprriinntt converts the contents of a given journal file into a
       human-readable  text format. Each line begins with aadddd or ddeell, to indi‐
       cate whether the record was added or deleted, and  continues  with  the
       resource record in master-file format.

       The  --cc  (compact)  option provides a mechanism to reduce the size of a
       journal by removing (most/all) transactions prior to the specified ser‐
       ial number. Note: this option _m_u_s_t _n_o_t be used while _n_a_m_e_d is  running,
       and  can  cause data loss if the zone file has not been updated to con‐
       tain the data being removed from the journal. Use with extreme caution.

       The --xx option causes additional data  about  the  journal  file  to  be
       printed  at  the  beginning  of  the  output  and  before each group of
       changes.

       The --uu (upgrade) and --dd (downgrade) options recreate the  journal  file
       with a modified format version.  The existing journal file is replaced.
       --dd  writes out the journal in the format used by versions of BIND up to
       9.16.11; --uu writes it out in the format used by versions since 9.16.13.
       (9.16.12 is omitted due to a journal-formatting bug in  that  release.)
       Note that these options _m_u_s_t _n_o_t be used while _n_a_m_e_d is running.

